== Client testing
- To test OPIS with users specific to ASTRO 100, we organised a user testing
  session with the ASTRO 100 tutors.
- This enabled us to get immediate feedback on the utility of OPIS with the
  tutors who will be using the application in the future.
- We conducted a user testing session with the ASTRO 100 tutors.
- This session lasted around 30 minutes.
- We instructed the tutors to try to answer the lab worksheet used in the ASTRO
  100 lab session using OPIS.
- We did not instruct the tutors in any way in how to use OPIS, rather letting
  them discover how to use the tool themselves.

=== Takeaways
- Generally, the feedback we obtained from the tutors was very positive.
- Key areas of improvement, where we noticed the tutors struggling in, or where
  OPIS could have been made more intuitive, were in:
  - Ensuring that there is a distinctive differentiation between planets and
    stars in the simulation.
    - We found that it was not always intuitive, especially for Kepler-16, to
      tell apart planets from stars.
    - This is important as in the provided lab worksheet there is a question
      which asks about how Kepler-16 is different to the Solar System (it is a
      binary star system).
  - Making the date picker more obvious.
    - We found that the users largely did not interact with the date picker.
    - This may have partially been due to a lack of a specific requirement to
      use it in answering the lab worksheet.
    - However, at the same time, the existing date picker was not distinctive,
      and did not suggest that it was a control which users can interact with.
